---
import Commit from './Commit.astro';
---

<Commit id="size" lane={1} class="py-20 lg:py-28">
  <div class="container-site rail-offset">
    <div class="max-w-2xl" data-reveal>
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-primary">Native</p>
      <h2 class="display mt-3 text-4xl leading-[1.02] sm:text-5xl">Native, not <em>a browser</em>.</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Most Git clients are a website wrapped in its own copy of a browser, which is why they take a moment to open
        and sit heavy on the disk. AngKorGit draws its window with the webview your OS already ships, and talks to the
        repository through libgit2 in the same process. It opens quickly, updates itself, and stays out of the way.
      </p>
    </div>

    <dl class="mt-14 grid max-w-3xl gap-6 border-t border-border-subtle pt-8 sm:grid-cols-3" data-reveal style={{ '--reveal-delay': '120ms' }}>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-faint">Engine</dt>
        <dd class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-snug text-foreground">Rust and libgit2, no shelling out for the things you do all day.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-faint">100k commits</dt>
        <dd class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-snug text-foreground">The graph and diffs are virtualized. Long histories scroll like short ones.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-faint">Cold start</dt>
        <dd class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-snug text-foreground">Open, splash, repo. The repository is read in process, no daemon to wait for.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</Commit>
